---
// Data Processing Terms (UK GDPR Article 28). Source: the approved draft for issue #119
// (approved by John, 24 Nov 2026). Replaces the 12-point Schedule in the Terms of Service:
// immediately for accounts opened from LEGAL_UPDATED, and for older accounts on
// DPA_EXISTING_ACCOUNTS_FROM (30 days' email notice first). Dates live in src/config/legal.ts.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { LEGAL_UPDATED, DPA_VERSION, DPA_EXISTING_ACCOUNTS_FROM } from '../config/legal';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Data Processing Terms"
  description="DriveSchoolPro's Data Processing Terms under UK GDPR Article 28: how we process pupil data for driving schools, security, sub-processors, breaches and deletion."
  modifiedDate="2026-11-24"
>
  <div class="bg-gray-100 py-3">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <Breadcrumbs items={[
        { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
        { label: 'Terms of Service', href: '/terms/' },
        { label: 'Data Processing Terms' }
      ]} />
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section bg-white">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-brand-navy mb-4">Data Processing Terms</h1>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-8">UK GDPR Article 28 · Version {DPA_VERSION} · Last updated: {LEGAL_UPDATED} · <a href="#changes" class="underline">What changed</a></p>
        <div class="prose max-w-none">

<p>These terms are between <strong>JOHN POWELL LTD</strong> (company number 05408918, registered in England and Wales, registered office 124 City Road, London, England, EC1V 2NX), trading as DriveSchoolPro ("<strong>we</strong>", the <strong>processor</strong>) and the driving instructor or driving school that holds a DriveSchoolPro account ("<strong>you</strong>", the <strong>controller</strong>). They form part of our <a href="/terms/">Terms of Service</a> and apply whenever we process personal data on your behalf. They replace the Schedule to the Terms of Service ("Data Processing Terms").</p>

<p><strong>When they apply.</strong> If you opened your account on or after {LEGAL_UPDATED}, these terms apply now. If you opened your account before {LEGAL_UPDATED}, they replace the earlier version of the Schedule on {DPA_EXISTING_ACCOUNTS_FROM}, and we will email you at least 30 days before. Until then the earlier version applies (it is shown at the end of our <a href="/terms/#data-processing">Terms of Service</a>), except that anything in these terms that only gives you more rights applies straight away.</p>

<p>"UK GDPR" means the UK GDPR as it forms part of the law of England and Wales, and the Data Protection Act 2018. "Personal data", "controller", "processor", "data subject", "personal data breach" and "special category data" have the meanings in UK GDPR.</p>

<h2 id="roles">1. Roles</h2>

<p>1.1 For personal data about your pupils, their parents or guardians, your instructors and staff, that you or they put into DriveSchoolPro, <strong>you are the controller and we are your processor</strong>.</p>

<p>1.2 For data about your account (your sign-in, billing, how you found us, support requests) and about visitors to our website, <strong>we are a controller in our own right</strong>. Our <a href="/privacy-policy/">Privacy Policy</a> covers that.</p>

<p>1.3 Where you are a sole trader, you are still the controller of your pupils' data and we are still your processor.</p>

<h2 id="details">2. Details of the processing (Article 28(3))</h2>

<div class="overflow-x-auto">
<table>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Subject matter</th><td>Providing the DriveSchoolPro service to you</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Duration</th><td>While your account is open, and then for the periods in clause 12</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Nature</th><td>Storing, organising, retrieving, displaying, sending messages, taking payments, generating AI briefings and summaries, backing up, exporting, erasing</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Purpose</th><td>Scheduling lessons; keeping pupil records and progress; messaging pupils and parents; invoicing and payments; storing documents; AI-written briefings and summaries; support</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Types of personal data</th><td>Name, email, phone and mobile number, date of birth, home and pickup addresses, driving licence number and expiry, theory and practical test details, lesson history, outcome and learning notes, progress ratings, payment and balance records, messages, communication preferences and consent records, emergency contact and parent details, uploaded documents, vehicle details, device tokens for push notifications, login records</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Special category data</th><td>Health information in the pupil medical notes field and in uploaded medical certificates, <strong>only where you choose to record it</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Other sensitive data</th><td>ID documents you upload; criminal-offence data is not a field in the product and must not be recorded</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Data subjects</th><td>Pupils (including some aged 16 and 17), parents and guardians, your instructors and staff</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h2 id="instructions">3. Your instructions</h2>

<p>3.1 We process the personal data <strong>only on your documented instructions</strong>: these terms, our Terms of Service, and your use of the service's features and settings. We will not process it for any other purpose, including our own marketing, advertising or product training.</p>

<p>3.2 If the law requires us to process it differently, we will tell you first unless the law forbids that.</p>

<p>3.3 If we think an instruction breaches data protection law, we will tell you.</p>

<p>3.4 The service lets you export data and archive pupils yourself. Archiving a pupil hides them but does not erase them. For anything else (for example erasing one pupil completely), email <a href="mailto:privacy@driveschoolpro.com">privacy@driveschoolpro.com</a>. We will carry out a written instruction within 30 days, or tell you why we cannot.</p>

<h2 id="confidentiality">4. Confidentiality</h2>

<p>4.1 We will make sure that everyone who can access your data for us is bound by a duty of confidentiality, and that their access is limited to what they need.</p>

<h2 id="security">5. Security (Article 32)</h2>

<p>We apply measures appropriate to the risk, including health and ID data and pupils under 18. The measures below are in place today, taken from our code and operating documents.</p>

<div class="overflow-x-auto">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>What we do</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Encryption in transit</td><td>HTTPS with HSTS on all app traffic; a content security policy and other browser security headers</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Encryption at rest</td><td>Our database provider (Neon) encrypts stored data with AES-256; nightly backups are stored in a private, versioned S3 bucket with server-side encryption and TLS-only access; school-supplied Twilio credentials are encrypted in our database with AES-256-GCM</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tenant separation</td><td>Every school's records carry the school's ID, and a database-layer check in our code refuses queries that are not scoped to it; pupil-facing queries also check the pupil</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Access control</td><td>Roles (owner, staff) with limits on what instructors can see; sign-in sessions expire after 7 days; our staff's support access to a school's account (impersonation) is excluded from product analytics</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Passwords and sign-in</td><td>Passwords are stored as bcrypt hashes (cost 12); sign-in attempts are throttled and accounts lock after repeated failures; password-reset and confirmation tokens expire and are single use</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Monitoring</td><td>Error monitoring (Sentry) with scrubbing of cookies, query strings, bodies and IP addresses; an audit log of important actions</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Backups and recovery</td><td>Nightly encrypted dump kept 30 days (daily) and 12 months (monthly); a database snapshot kept 21 days; a weekly automated test restore</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Data minimisation with AI</td><td>Only a pupil's first name and lesson content are sent; the medical notes field is never sent</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Development</td><td>Changes are reviewed and tested before release; dependency advisories are checked in the build</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Resilience</td><td>Hosted in London on a managed platform</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>We rely on the certifications of our providers, which they publish.</p>

<p><strong>Known limits.</strong> We want these terms to describe the service as it is today:</p>
<ul>
<li>Uploaded documents are stored with Vercel Blob using public, hard-to-guess addresses rather than private, expiring links. Anyone who has an address can open the file.</li>
<li>When we erase an organisation, the database pointer to a document is removed but the stored file is not deleted automatically. Clause 12.3 says what we do instead.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="sub-processors">6. Sub-processors (Article 28(2) and (4))</h2>

<p>6.1 <strong>General authorisation.</strong> You authorise the sub-processors listed on our <a href="/subprocessors/">sub-processors page</a> at the date of these terms.</p>

<p>6.2 <strong>Change notice.</strong> We will email the account owner at least <strong>30 days</strong> before we add or replace a sub-processor that can receive pupil data. If we have to replace a provider in an emergency (for example it fails or has a security problem), we will tell you as soon as we can and give the 30 days where we can.</p>

<p>6.3 <strong>Objection.</strong> You may object on reasonable data-protection grounds within the notice period. We will try to find a way to keep serving you without that sub-processor. If we cannot, you may close your account before the change and we will refund any fees paid for the period after it. Using the service after the change means you accept it.</p>

<p>6.4 <strong>Flow-down.</strong> We put data protection terms in place with each sub-processor giving at least the protection in these terms, and we remain responsible to you for what they do. Some providers publish standard terms that we accept, which give them shorter notice of their own sub-processors than we give you (for example Vercel allows five calendar days to object). We will pass on what we learn.</p>

<p>6.5 Some providers, like Stripe, also act as independent controllers for some of their own purposes (fraud checks, legal compliance). If you connect your own Stripe account, you also enter a direct agreement with Stripe.</p>

<h2 id="transfers">7. International transfers</h2>

<p>7.1 Our app servers and main database are in London, and our database backups are in Ireland. Uploaded files are stored by Vercel. Several sub-processors are US companies, and the sub-processors page says which.</p>

<p>7.2 We transfer personal data outside the UK only where a lawful mechanism applies: UK adequacy regulations; the UK Extension to the EU-US Data Privacy Framework for certified US providers; or the UK International Data Transfer Addendum or Agreement with the provider. We keep copies of these and will send you a copy of a sub-processor's safeguard on request.</p>

<h2 id="assistance">8. Helping you with your obligations</h2>

<p>8.1 <strong>Data subject requests.</strong> If a pupil, parent or instructor asks us to use their rights, we will not answer substantively; we will pass it to you within <strong>5 working days</strong>. We will help you respond within the legal month, including exports, corrections and deletion (clause 3.4).</p>

<p>8.2 <strong>Personal data breaches.</strong> We will tell you <strong>without undue delay and in any case within 48 hours</strong> after we become aware of a personal data breach affecting your data. We will tell you what we know: what happened, the likely categories and numbers of people and records, the likely consequences and what we are doing, and a contact. We will give more as we learn it. You decide whether to tell the ICO (within 72 hours of when you become aware, where the risk requires it) and the individuals. We will help with both. Our sub-processors tell us on their own terms, some of them without a fixed deadline, so the 48 hours runs from when we become aware.</p>

<p>8.3 <strong>Security and impact assessments.</strong> We will provide the information you reasonably need for a data protection impact assessment or consultation with the ICO, for example on AI briefings and on health data. We can give you a description of the processing, the sub-processor list and our security measures.</p>

<p>8.4 We do not charge for this help unless it needs unusual effort, in which case we agree the cost with you first.</p>

<h2 id="ai">9. AI processing</h2>

<p>9.1 AI briefings and summaries send the pupil's first name and the content of notes to Anthropic's Claude model through the Vercel AI Gateway. If Anthropic is unavailable, the gateway can send the request to the same model run by Amazon Bedrock or Google Vertex AI (see the sub-processors page). We do not send the medical notes field.</p>

<p>9.2 We do not use your data to train AI models.</p>

<p>9.3 A person reviews AI output. It is advice to the instructor, and no decision about a pupil is made automatically.</p>

<p>9.4 You must not put health or other special category details into free-text notes. If you do, they will be sent with the note.</p>

<p>9.5 There is no setting to turn AI features off for a school. If you need that, tell us.</p>

<h2 id="your-responsibilities">10. Your responsibilities</h2>

<p>You confirm that you (a) have a lawful basis for the personal data you put into DriveSchoolPro, and an Article 9 condition for any health data; (b) have told pupils and parents how their data is used, for example through a privacy notice for pupils; (c) will not record data you do not need; (d) will keep sign-in details secure and remove people who leave; and (e) have the necessary consent before using any optional messaging.</p>

<h2 id="audits">11. Audits and information</h2>

<p>We will make available the information you reasonably need to show we meet Article 28. You may carry out one audit a year on 30 days' notice, during working hours, without disrupting the service, at your own cost, and subject to confidentiality. We may satisfy the request first with written answers or a summary of third-party reports from our providers.</p>

<h2 id="end-of-service">12. End of the service: return and deletion</h2>

<p>12.1 You can export your data at any time from the app.</p>

<p>12.2 If you close your account, we keep your data for <strong>30 days</strong>, so you can export it or change your mind. After that we erase or anonymise it, except: (a) financial records, which we must keep for <strong>6 years</strong>; (b) backups, which expire on their own cycle (daily copies after 30 days, monthly copies after 12 months); (c) records of the erasure itself.</p>

<p>12.3 Files you uploaded are not removed from storage automatically at the end of the account. We will delete them by hand within 30 days of a written request to <a href="mailto:privacy@driveschoolpro.com">privacy@driveschoolpro.com</a>.</p>

<h2 id="general">13. Liability and the rest</h2>

<p>13.1 The limits on liability in our Terms of Service apply to these terms.</p>

<p>13.2 If a clause here conflicts with our Terms of Service about personal data, these terms win.</p>

<p>13.3 English law and the courts of England and Wales apply.</p>

<h2 id="changes">What changed</h2>

<p><strong>Version {DPA_VERSION}, {LEGAL_UPDATED}:</strong> these terms replace the 12-point Schedule at the end of our Terms of Service. They keep its main promises (you are the controller; 30 days' notice of a new sub-processor; breach notice within 48 hours; one audit a year on 30 days' notice) and add the full details of the processing, our security measures and their known limits, how sub-processor terms flow down, AI processing, and what happens to your data when your account ends.</p>

        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
